WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.771
Executing generated code.

2
00:00:02.051 --> 00:00:05.400
When the output is code, the strongest grader is to run it.

3
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:24.091
Chen and colleagues at OpenAI argued this in the 2021 paper that introduced the HumanEval benchmark: match-based metrics can’t account for the huge space of programs that are functionally equivalent to a reference solution, so they evaluated functional correctness instead,

4
00:00:24.371 --> 00:00:28.019
counting a sample as correct if it passes a set of unit tests.

5
00:00:28.299 --> 00:00:32.821
They pointed out that this mirrors how developers already judge code, through tests.

6
00:00:33.101 --> 00:00:41.549
The paper also introduced the standard way to report it, pass@k: the probability that at least one of k sampled solutions passes.

7
00:00:41.829 --> 00:00:51.536
To estimate it with low variance, generate n samples (n at least k), count the c that pass, and compute 1 minus C(n minus c, k) over C(n, k).

8
00:00:51.816 --> 00:00:59.261
The obvious shortcut, 1 minus (1 minus the pass rate) to the k, is biased, which the paper shows in an appendix.

9
00:00:59.541 --> 00:01:02.741
Executing model-written code is a security decision.

10
00:01:03.021 --> 00:01:10.211
Generated code can delete files, read secrets from your environment, open network connections, or loop forever.

11
00:01:10.491 --> 00:01:14.267
The HumanEval authors built a sandbox for exactly this reason.

12
00:01:14.547 --> 00:01:31.549
In practice, run generated code in a disposable container or a managed sandbox with no credentials, no network unless the test needs it, a CPU and memory limit, and a timeout, and grade what the run left behind (test results, files written, exit code).

13
00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:41.003
Anthropic’s agent-evals write-up makes the same point for agents generally: grade the outcome in the environment, not the transcript’s description of it.
